# Trust-region reflective

Trust-region reflective (TRF) minimizes nonlinear least-squares objectives with
box bounds. It combines distance-to-bound scaling, an explicit trust radius,
reflected trial steps, and a rank-aware dense SVD solve.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/trust_region_reflective/struct.TrustRegionReflective.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                                  |
  | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
  | Objective   | A smooth sum of squared residuals, or an adapted robust loss |
  | Evaluations | `Residual` and `Jacobian`                                    |
  | Search      | Local, from one starting point                               |
  | Constraints | Box bounds only                                              |
  | Parameters  | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64`                 |

## When to use it

Consider TRF for small and medium dense bounded fits, especially when the
Jacobian may be rank deficient or some parameters must be fixed. For sparse
Jacobians, compare [bounded LM](../bounded-levenberg-marquardt/).

The scaling follows [Branch, Coleman, and Li
(1999)](#ref-branch-coleman-li-1999), with trust-region parameter selection
related to [Moré (1978)](#ref-more-1978). Candidate selection and radius updates
follow SciPy's dense TRF variant. This is a local method; bound-aware steps do
not ensure a global minimum.

## How it works

The solver scales free coordinates by their distance to the relevant bound and
adds a nonnegative curvature correction to the linear residual model. A
pure-Rust one-sided Jacobi SVD factors the augmented model directly, without
forming normal equations. Singular directions below the rank cutoff are omitted.
The minimum-norm Gauss–Newton step is used when it fits inside the radius;
otherwise a safeguarded secular solve limits its length.

When a proposed step meets a bound, the solver compares a truncated step, a
first-boundary reflection, and a scaled-gradient step. It accepts finite
objective decreases and updates the radius using actual versus predicted
reduction. Rejected trials reuse the factorization. Finite equal bounds fix
coordinates, which are eliminated internally.

## Example

The unconstrained residual minimum is `[3.0, 2.0]`, outside the declared box.
The bounded solution is `[2.0, 2.0]` and has cost `0.5`.

<!-- compile: solver-trust-region-reflective -->
```rust
use basin::{
    BoxBounds, Constrained, ConstraintSet, DenseMatrix, Executor, Jacobian,
    Residual, TrustRegionReflective,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Fit;

impl Residual for Fit {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = Vec<f64>;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn residual(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<Self::Output, Self::Error> {
        Ok(vec![x[0] - 3.0, 2.0 * (x[1] - 2.0)])
    }
}

impl Jacobian for Fit {
    type Jacobian = DenseMatrix;

    fn jacobian(&self, _: &Self::Param) -> Result<Self::Jacobian, Self::Error> {
        Ok(DenseMatrix::from_row_slice(2, 2, &[1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 2.0]))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let solver = TrustRegionReflective::new()
        .with_absolute_scaled_gradient_tolerance(1e-8);
    let constraints = ConstraintSet::new().with_bounds(BoxBounds::new(
        Some(vec![0.0, 0.0]),
        Some(vec![2.0, 4.0]),
    ));
    let problem = Constrained::new(Fit, constraints);
    let result = Executor::from_start(problem, solver, vec![0.5, 0.5])
        .max_iter(100)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!((result.param()[0] - 2.0).abs() < 1e-6);
    assert!((result.param()[1] - 2.0).abs() < 1e-6);
    assert!((result.cost() - 0.5).abs() < 1e-6);
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Declare the complete box

Attach a box-only constraint declaration to the residual problem. Finite starts
are projected strictly inside free intervals. Equal finite bounds fix a
parameter; an all-fixed problem evaluates residuals once and finishes without a
Jacobian. Signed infinite bounds are supported. Bounds and residual dimensions
must remain fixed during a solve.

Use `BoundedFiniteDiff` when numerical Jacobians must respect bounds. Invalid
shapes, reversed or NaN bounds, non-finite starts, and invalid settings panic. A
free interval without a representable interior fails before any callback.

### Interpret rank and stopping settings

`with_rank_tolerance` controls which singular directions the dense solve
retains. Storage is `O(mn + n²)` for `m` residuals and `n` parameters; there is
no sparse or large-scale subspace path.

`with_absolute_scaled_gradient_tolerance` tests `max |v ⊙ Jᵀr|` over free
coordinates, with default `1e-8`. This metric accounts for bounds but remains
sensitive to units. Optional observed cost and step checks combine with OR and
inspect accepted iterates. Small changes alone do not certify stationarity.

Non-finite trial residuals cause rejection and radius contraction. Numerical
breakdowns or exhausted retries produce `SolverFailed`; an unchanged trial after
finite equal-cost rejection can produce `NumericalNoProgress`. Inspect the
report, feasibility, and fit quality, and keep budgets on the executor.

## Implementation

Trust-region reflective publishes matching parameters and cost through
`PointState`. It computes the gradient internally; the state does not expose it
through `GradientState`. Residuals, Jacobians, and model caches belong to the
solver. Fresh runs reset them, while exact solver-and-state checkpoints retain
them.

For ordinary residuals, the reported cost is `0.5 * sum(r[i]^2)`. The solver
requires `Residual` and `Jacobian`, without a `CostFunction` implementation.
Wrapping the problem in
[`RobustLeastSquares`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/core/least_squares/struct.RobustLeastSquares.html)
selects a robust loss and residual scale. The adapter supplies a safeguarded
model, while costs and convergence use the robust objective and its gradient.
Custom Jacobians additionally need `ScaleRowsInPlace` for robust losses.

Supported dense pairs are `Vec<F>`/`DenseMatrix<F>`,
`nalgebra::DVector<F>`/`DMatrix<F>`, `ndarray::Array1<F>`/`Array2<F>`, and
`faer::Col<F>`/`Mat<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. The default `Vec` path needs
no BLAS/LAPACK provider.

All backends share the internal dense SVD kernel through entry access. Sparse
Jacobians are unsupported. Use `Executor::run_with_solver` to retain the solver
and inspect native convergence diagnostics, including whether an all-fixed
problem caused termination.

Reference comparisons use [SciPy 1.16.2's dense
TRF](https://github.com/scipy/scipy/blob/v1.16.2/scipy/optimize/_lsq/trf.py).
Fixed-coordinate elimination and minimum-norm interior steps under rank
deficiency are Basin extensions; trajectories need not agree.

